A||||4||Atlas||Engine cuts off at freeway exits
Z||000000||Atlas||05-24-99||10:58 AM||||My 85 SE 6 cylinder automatic runs great on the freeway, but starts to run shaky and then cuts off after I exit and get into traffic. (When coming to a stop the rpm's won't go below 1500, then stammering starts.)  After it shuts off it won't start again until it sits for awhile. Same thing now happens after extended runs on surface streets (5-10 miles.)  Coolant and oil temp/pressure seem fine.  Any thoughts?
Z||000001||bonnovy||05-26-99||06:37 PM||||i hope someone can answer this. i have an 89 corsica with a 2.8l v6 that has the same problem. after a run on the freeway, coming to an off ramp and just about stopped it shutters and dies. in my case it starts right up but as soon as i put it in gear it dies. does this about 5 or 6 times then runs great and goes. also at times on the freeway when i kick it into passing gear it hesitates then backfires then takes off.
Z||000002||theogre||05-26-99||06:46 PM||||there is a known problem with reliability in the TH125 3 speed automatic that might be part or all of this. (I had to think about this one for a bit.... It happens to allot of people at red lights too.)<p>The problem is the TCC lockup solinoid doesn't unlock when it should. This is usually a heat related problem.<p>The fix is to pull off the side cover of the tranny, and replace the TCC solinoid. In many cases this can be done without pulling the tranny.<p>To be sure if this is your problem, unplug the TCC control line from the tranny. It's low on the front side of the tranny if I remember right.<p>This will keep the ECM from locking the torq converter. If your problem goes away, replace the TCC solinoid. DO NOT continue to drive the car with the solinoid disabled. You can burn up the tranny without the lockup.
Z||000003||Atlas||05-27-99||11:35 AM||||We're going to replace the solenoid this weekend.  Should run about $44.62 + $8.00 for a gasket, priced at the local dealership.  I really love this car, but haven't been able to convince my wife to spend any money on fixing it (we suspected major work to the tranny.)  Thanks for getting us back together (me and the car, that is!)
Z||000004||Lynx||05-28-99||10:49 AM||||Orge is Right ON like usual. My mom has a 87 grand am with a 3.0V6 and the 3speed auto and it did the same EXACT thing and it was the silenoid in the tranny just need the part and the side cover gasket. Its a EASY fix.
